   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Now let me analyze the observations more carefully. Looking at the training metrics and logs:\n\n**Key observations:**\n\n1. **Very high label noise fraction (42%)**: This is unusual. The config has `label_noise_fraction: 0.42`, which means 42% of training labels are intentionally flipped.\n\n2. **Training loss behavior**: The training loss decreases very slowly, starting at ~0.689 and ending at ~0.684, a decrease of only ~0.005 over 20 epochs. This is very small progress.\n\n3. **Validation accuracy**: The validation accuracy is relatively stable around 0.82-0.83, with some noise (drops to 0.76 at epoch 4, then recovers). The best accuracy is ~0.8294 (epoch 12).\n\n4. **Minimal learning**: For binary classification on Adult dataset, starting from 0.689 loss (which is close to random guessing for a balanced binary classification) and only decreasing to 0.684 suggests the model is barely learning anything.\n\nLet me check if 42% label noise is unreasonable or expected. The issue is that 42% label noise is extremely high for a training dataset. This would mean more than 40% of the labels are wrong, making it nearly impossible for the model to learn the true pattern. The model is essentially trying to fit corrupted labels, which explains why the loss barely decreases.\n\nThis appears to be a **silent fault** - the training runs to completion without errors, but the configuration is fundamentally broken. A label noise fraction of 42% is far higher than any reasonable value (typically used for robustness experiments with values like 0.1 or 0.2). This looks like an accidental misconfiguration.\n\nLet me verify by checking what a reasonable value might be:\n\nThe fact that the training runs to completion without errors but shows minimal learning progress is suspicious. 42% label noise is about 4x higher than typical label noise experiments (which use 5-10%). This appears to be a misconfiguration.\n\nLet me check the reference config or any hints:",
